The problem is now identified. ✅

From your Events:

Plain Text
Container myapp failed liveness probe, will be restarted
 
Readiness probe failed:
dial tcp 10.x.x.x:80: connect: connection refused
Root Cause

Your application is running on:

Plain Text
Tomcat -> Port 8080

We can see that in the logs:

Plain Text
Starting ProtocolHandler ["http-nio-8080"]

But Kubernetes health checks are trying to access:

Plain Text
Port 80

Error:

Plain Text
Readiness probe failed:
Get "http://10.244.0.16:80/"
connect: connection refused

So Kubernetes thinks the application is unhealthy and keeps restarting it.

Fix Deployment / Helm Chart

Check your Helm chart:

Shell
cat myapp/templates/deployment.yaml

Look for:

YAML
livenessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /
port: 80
 
readinessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /
port: 80

Change both to:

YAML
livenessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /
port: 8080
initialDelaySeconds: 60
periodSeconds: 10
 
readinessProbe:
httpGet:
path: /
port: 8080
initialDelaySeconds: 30
periodSeconds: 10
Also Check Container Port

Make sure:

YAML
ports:
- containerPort: 8080

not:

YAML
ports:
- containerPort: 80
Apply Changes

If using Helm:

Shell
helm upgrade myapp1 ./myapp

If using ArgoCD:

Shell
git add .
git commit -m "Fixed probes to use port 8080"
git push origin master

Then:

Plain Text
ArgoCD → SYNC
Verify
Shell
kubectl get pods

Expected:

Plain Text
myapp1-xxxxx 1/1 Running
myapp1-yyyyy 1/1 Running
myapp1-zzzzz 1/1 Running
Quick Check

Run:

Shell
grep -R "port: 80" myapp/templates

I am almost certain you'll find the liveness/readiness probes configured to use port 80, while your Tomcat app listens on 8080. Changing the probe port to 8080 should stop the CrashLoopBackOff.

